:"higher in AD","nucleus_padj":0.877944,"nucleus_log2fc":0.0802002,"nucleus_score":0.379254,"pct_ad":0.289053,"pct_control":0.27965,"donor_test":"not significant","donor_padj":0.919006,"donor_log2fc":0.187795,"donor_lfcse":0.276806,"donor_log2fc_shrunk":0.00305701},{"cell_type":"OPC","gene_symbol":"GRID2","gene_symbol_current":"GRID2","gene_id":"ENSG00000152208","nucleus_tested":true,"nucleus_significant":false,"nucleus_direction":"unclear","nucleus_padj":0.6561,"nucleus_log2fc":-0.0247924,"nucleus_score":0.901177,"pct_ad":0.925432,"pct_control":0.940996,"donor_test":"not significant","donor_padj":0.993604,"donor_log2fc":0.0196767,"donor_lfcse":0.0629629,"donor_log2fc_shrunk":0.000948754},{"cell_type":"Oli","gene_symbol":"GRID2","gene_symbol_current":"GRID2","gene_id":"ENSG00000152208","nucleus_tested":true,"nucleus_significant":false,"nucleus_direction":"higher in AD","nucleus_padj":0.627128,"nucleus_log2fc":0.0187594,"nucleus_score":0.657272,"pct_ad":0.247832,"pct_control":0.239783,"donor_test":"not significant","donor_padj":0.990066,"donor_log2fc":-0.029324,"donor_lfcse":0.419545,"donor_log2fc_shrunk":-0.000166226}],"modules":[{"cell_type":"Ast","gene_id":"ENSG00000152208","module":"red","kme":0.272088,"hub":false,"module_direction":"lower in AD","module_padj":0.32433,"module_significant":false},{"cell_type":"In","gene_id":"ENSG00000152208","module":"blue","kme":0.177651,"hub":false,"module_direction":"higher in AD","module_padj":0.906476,"module_significant":false},{"cell_type":"OPC","gene_id":"ENSG00000152208","module":"salmon","kme":0.293138,"hub":false,"module_direction":"lower in AD","module_padj":0.683527,"module_significant":false}],"degas_bulk_de":false,"drug_target":false}},"description":[{"gene_id":"ENSG00000152208","entrez_id":["2895"],"uniprot":["O43424"],"ncbi_summary":"The protein encoded by this gene is a member of the family of ionotropic glutamate receptors which are the predominant excitatory neurotransmitter receptors in the mammalian brain. The encoded protein is a multi-pass membrane protein that is expressed selectively in cerebellar Purkinje cells. A point mutation in the mouse ortholog, associated with the phenotype named 'lurcher', in the heterozygous state leads to ataxia resulting from selective, cell-autonomous apoptosis of cerebellar Purkinje cells during postnatal development. Mice homozygous for this mutation die shortly after birth from massive loss of mid- and hindbrain neurons during late embryogenesis. This protein also plays a role in synapse organization between parallel fibers and Purkinje cells. Alternate splicing results in multiple transcript variants encoding distinct isoforms. Mutations in this gene cause cerebellar ataxia in humans. [provided by RefSeq, Apr 2014].","ncbi_source":"NCBI Gene summary (RefSeq), via MyGene.info","uniprot_function":[{"accession":"O43424","text":"Member of the ionotropic glutamate receptor family, which plays a crucial role in synaptic organization and signal transduction in the central nervous system (PubMed:34936451, PubMed:27418511, PubMed:40957579). Although it shares structural features with ionotropic glutamate receptors, does not bind glutamate as a primary ligand (PubMed:34936451). Promotes synaptogenesis and mediates the D-serine-dependent long term depression signals and AMPA receptor endocytosis of cerebellar parallel fiber-Purkinje cell (PF-PC) synapses through the NRX1B-CBLN1-GRID2 triad complex (PubMed:27418511). In the presence of neurexins and cerebellins, forms cation-selective channels that are proposed to be gated by glycine and D-serine (PubMed:34936451). Cation-selective ion channel activity can be triggered by GRM1 in Purkinje cells (PubMed:24357660, PubMed:27276689)."}],"uniprot_source":"UniProtKB/Swiss-Prot Function, release 2026_03","retrieved":"2026-10-21"}],"structures":[],"alphafold_accessions":[]}